sri lanka has reached crisis point  thousands of protesters have stormed the  presidential palace and taught the prime  minister's home so what happens now both  the prime minister and the president  have agreed to resign  sri lanka is facing its worst economic  crisis since it achieved independence in  1948. the island nation of 22 million  people has been left in financial  turmoil after economic mismanagement  and the impact of the pandemic and now  confusion over the change of leadership  their constitution usually dictates if  the president resigns the prime minister  in this case reynold wick remasinga will  step in and act in the office of  president  but the protesters have demanded both  the president and the prime minister  step down  however it's parliament and not the  people that elect sri lanka's next  president  the sri lankan parliament has said  they'll elect a new president by the end  of july  cabinet members say they'll also resign  once an all-party government is formed  only time will tell whether the handover  of power will be smooth or whether the  outcome will be accepted by protesters  and what about sri lanka's financial  woes  the international monetary fund said it  was hoping for a resolution to sri  lanka's political turmoil  to allow talks about a potential bailout  package to resume  there's unlikely to be any real progress  on that front until the country has a  legitimate president and prime minister  in place  but whether a bailout is the answer long  term remains to be seen  a tourism industry badly damaged by  suicide bombings and covert 19  severe foreign exchange shortages  limiting fuel food and medicine imports  record high inflation and vast  accumulating debts have all helped pile  pressure on an already cash-strapped  population  like many countries sri lanka has been  hit by the global fallout of the war in  ukraine and the ensuing fuel crisis  in a sense it's a bit of a canary in the  coal mine and the eyes of the world are  trained on sri lanka to see what it does  next  many people here blame the rajapaksa  political dynasty for the country's  economic collapse  the family has been in control of sri  lanka for two decades  months of protest in sri lanka reached  fever pitch when thousands of people  stormed the government district in  colombo when protesters broke into the  president's house many were angered by  the opulence of the soaring ceilings  four poster bed and vast marble bathroom  an indication of the gulf between the  countries rich  the resignation of the president and the  prime minister  may make many sri lankans feel that  months of hardship and demonstrations  have not been in vain  but what happens next with sri lanka's  economic future still hangs in the  balance
 

